// Conspiracy Thriller: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== conspiracy-thriller.f01 | feature | three-act
title: Forty-Three Articles
genre: conspiracy-thriller + historical-drama
tone: Foggy, feverish and coldly lucid
era: 1678 | period
setting: Restoration London in a season of panic | real | city
logline: A magistrate's clerk who takes down a sworn statement about a vast plot to murder the king realises, as the city goes mad with fear, that the only conspiracy is the one being run by the man who swore it.
premise: A disgraced clergyman with a prodigious memory and a wide chin swears before a magistrate to forty-three articles: assassins, poison, silver bullets, a foreign army. Three weeks later the magistrate is found dead in a ditch with his own sword through him. After that nobody needs evidence. {abel}, the dead man's clerk, wrote the articles out fair. He is good at comparing documents.
synopsis: The first act is a deposition and a corpse. {abel} notices, copying, that the informer {the_doctor} spells a foreign name three ways and has men in two cities on one day. His master, {sir_humphrey}, sees it too, and is frightened in a way {abel} does not understand, and then is dead. London buys daggers engraved with his name; ladies carry pistols in their muffs; a mob burns effigies by the hundred. The second act is a conspiracy thriller turned inside out: {abel} investigates, but what he keeps finding is nothing. The letters cited do not exist. The inn where the plotters met has no such room. A witness, {stroud}, a horse thief, is learning his testimony by heart from the first. Each proof of emptiness is useless, because doubting the plot is itself evidence of being in it. A great lord, {the_earl}, who does not believe a word, finds it the most useful thing that ever happened to his party. Men are tried. {abel} sits in court and hears the judge sum up against a harmless old priest he has himself questioned; the jury does not leave the box. He takes his collation of the forty-three articles to the one man powerful enough to act, and understands, mid-sentence, that the man already knows. Who killed {sir_humphrey} the film does not decide; it offers three answers and shows what each would have needed. In the third act {abel} chooses something small. He cannot stop it. He can keep a true record. He writes out, each night, what was sworn and what was so, in two columns, and hides the book in the binding of a law report. Fifteen men die. Three years on, the fever passes as fevers do; the informer is caught in a perjury so plain that even a judge can see it, and is whipped through the streets {abel} walks to work. Nobody asks for the book. He leaves it to a library.
question: What can one accurate man do in a city that needs to believe?
themes: panic as a political resource; evidence against appetite; the record as resistance
audience: Adults who like their paranoia historical
viewpoint: With {abel}; the camera reads over his shoulder
stakes: Innocent lives by the dozen, his own if he is noticed, and whether the truth of it will survive at all
conflict: A clerk with a collation against an informer with a story everyone wants, and against the powerful men for whom the lie is convenient
ending: He cannot stop the trials. He keeps the true account in secret. The liar is exposed years later; the dead stay dead.
experience: Creeping dread, the vertigo of proof that proves nothing, and a small hard consolation
look: Fog, torchlight, effigies; paper everywhere, depositions, broadsides, playing cards with the plot on them; one ditch
sound: Quills, a mob's chant coming and going on the wind, a court crier. A whip counted at the end.
m: expose | community | loyalty-vs-truth | bittersweet
@ abel | Abel Marsh | lead | protagonist | human | he/him | 30 | The truth on paper, somewhere | He copied the lie fair with his own hand | Doubt is treason this year | Precise, timid, obstinate | From copyist to secret chronicler | Hesitant speech; fluent on the page | Rusty black, ink-stained cuffs, spectacles
@ the_doctor | The Doctor | co-lead | antagonist | human | he/him | 29 | To be believed, and kept | He has been expelled from everything | His story must grow to stay alive | Brazen, wounded, gifted with recall | From beggar to the saviour of the nation to the cart's tail | A bray, pulpit periods | A bishop's silk he is not entitled to, a huge chin
@ sir_humphrey | Sir Humphrey | supporting | mentor | human | he/him | 57 | To have done his duty and no more | He took the deposition | He knows something he should not | Upright, melancholy, afraid | Alive for one act; a question for the rest | Grave, courteous | A justice's band, a walking sword
@ the_earl | The Earl | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 57 | The succession changed | He has waited twenty years for a lever | The lever is a lie and he knows it | Brilliant, small, in pain, pitiless | Unchanged; he rides it until it throws him | Silken, amused | A tap in his side for an old wound, a great wig
@ stroud | Captain Stroud | featured | foil | human | he/him | 28 | A pension | The first liar is being paid | He has to remember his lines | Handsome, shameless | From horse thief to king's evidence | Glib, with rehearsed tears | A borrowed sword and a second-hand title
~ abel - sir_humphrey | clerk and master | a deposition copied | a death he cannot explain
~ abel - the_doctor | copyist and informer | he writes down every word | he keeps the other column
~ abel - the_earl | petitioner and great lord | he brings the proof | he sees it was never wanted
# The Articles | INT | Magistrate's Parlour | DAY | abel, sir_humphrey, the_doctor | Take it down | Forty-three articles, sworn. The informer never hesitates. {abel}'s pen slows at a name spelled three ways. His master has gone grey | The plot is sworn | 6 | props=deposition
# The Articles | EXT | Ditch, Primrose Hill | DAY | abel | Find him | His master, face down, his own sword through him, his shoes clean. By night there are daggers for sale with his name on | The magistrate is dead | 5 | crowd=40, vfx=corpse
# Nothing | INT | Tavern, Upper Floor | DAY | abel | Find no room | The inn where the plotters met. There is no upstairs parlour. There never was. The landlord begs him not to say so | | 5 | props=floor plan
# Nothing | INT | Lodging | NIGHT | abel, stroud | Overhear a rehearsal | Through a wall: a second witness learning the first's testimony aloud, being corrected on dates | | 4 |
# Nothing | INT | Court | DAY | abel, the_doctor | Watch a trial | An old priest {abel} has questioned himself. The judge sums up for the Crown and the mob. The jury does not leave the box | An innocent man is condemned | 6 | crowd=200
# Nothing | INT | Great House, Closet | NIGHT | abel, the_earl | Bring the proof | Two columns: sworn, and so. The earl reads it with pleasure, as a connoisseur. He asks who else has seen it. {abel} understands | The powerful already know | 6 | props=collation
# The Book | INT | Chambers, By Candle | NIGHT | abel | Keep the record | Each night, two columns. The book goes into the binding of a law report. Outside, a procession burns a pope of straw | | 4 | props=hidden book, vfx=effigy burning
# The Book | EXT | Street, Cart's Tail | DAY | abel, the_doctor | See him whipped | Three years on. The informer at the cart's tail, counted strokes. Their eyes meet. {abel} goes on to work | The liar is exposed | 4 | crowd=150, stunts=flogging
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from the fabricated plot of 1678 and the unexplained death of the magistrate who took the deposition, with invented people
